  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Methyl 4-Formylbenzoate and the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, as this compound is identified as a target of Malme-3M, which in turn is a target of the compound. The 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 is a known component of the proteasome, an essential complex involved in protein degradation. The evidence indicates that Methyl 4-Formylbenzoate may act as a modulator or inhibitor of the proteasome, potentially through interactions with Malme-3M, which is a target of the compound. The high scores in both paths suggest a strong association, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be elucidated. The evidence supports a possible regulatory role of Methyl 4-Formylbenzoate in the proteasome pathway, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the functional relationship.",
